"explanation": "Household electrical circuits are designed so that each outlet receives the full supply voltage (typically 120V or 230V) independently. This is achieved by wiring the outlets in parallel. If the appliances were connected in series, the voltage would be divided among them, and turning off one appliance would break the circuit for all others. The fact that turning one appliance on or off does not affect the performance of the others is the defining characteristic of a parallel circuit connection.",
